I think that's working correctly, even if there are dependencies of the members injection binding that transitively have local multibinding contributions. @netdpb does something like this make sense to you?

We can obviously clean this up before submitting.

I think we want to leave this as it currently is in the PR.

If we start checking if a binding is defined locally and hence conflicts with a previously resolved binding in `localDependencyChecker.dependsOnLocalBindings()` this makes determining the owning component of a binding in `Resolver.getOwningComponent()` more challenging. Checking if a binding is defined locally in a separate method that can be reused in other places makes things more composable and straightforward. 

@netdpb @ronshapiro Does this PR currently make sense or is there some reason why we'd want to check for binding conflicts in `dependsOnLocalBindings()`?

Hmm. Shouldn't we check to see if any of the _transitive_ dependencies are redefined locally, even for contribution bindings? I think we have a problem even with this:

```
@Component(modules = ParentModule.class) interface Parent {
  Foo foo();
  Child.Builder child();
}

@Subcomponent(modules = ChildModule.class) interface Child {
  Foo foo();
}

@Module(subcomponents = Child.class) abstract class ParentModule {
  @Provides static Foo foo(Bar bar) { ... }
  @Provides static Bar bar() { ... }
}

@Module abstract class ChildModule {
  @Provides static Bar bar() { ... }
}
```

The `Bar` binding in `ChildModule` _should_ conflict with the one in `ParentModule`, but it doesn't.

In any case, we have to be careful with this PR because people's components might suddenly report previously hidden errors.

We must check the parent here otherwise we could end up resolving a previously unresolved binding in the parent using a binding thats defined in the child:

```
@Component(modules = ParentModule.class) interface Parent {
  Child child();
}

@Subcomponent(modules = ChildModule.class) interface Child {
  Bar bar()
  Foo foo()
}

@Module abstract class ParentModule {
  @Provides static Foo foo(Bar bar) { ... }
}

@Module abstract class ChildModule {
  @Provides static Bar bar() { ... }
}
```

You're right, this method shouldn't care if the parent bindings are empty. I've removed this check in 4f78aab. 

However, in addition to the case you mentioned above, `LocalDependencyChecker` is also used to determine the owning component of a binding in `getOwningComponent()`. Now that we're checking for local contribution bindings here, we incorrectly determine the owning component of the `Object` binding to be `Child`.

```
@Module
class ParentModule { 
  @Provides Object needsString(String string) { return new Object; } 
}

@Component(modules = ParentModule.class)
interface Parent { Child child(); }

@Module
class ChildModule {
  @Provides String string() { return "Hi!" }
}

@Subcomponent(modules = ChildModule.class)
interface Child {
  String string();
  Object object();
}
```

To address this I think we need to add some additional logic to `getOwningComponent()`. Does that make sense? At the moment Travis will fail on this case.
